Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH (@met-handbook: 0, is #book, #part-of @met-sources) { r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN
NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods, chapter 2, Measurement Process Characterization, with supporting chapters on process modelling and exploratory data analysis. [Chapter 2 and detailed contents](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/mpc.htm) · [Handbook contents](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/).

Read chapter 2 completely through its linked sections: characterization, measurement control, calibration, gauge repeatability and reproducibility studies, uncertainty analysis, and case studies. Work the supplied numerical examples and consult their references. Use the process-modelling material for regression assumptions and residual diagnostics when the calibration reading calls for it.

This is a substantial open technical handbook, not a single summary webpage. Record the section title and stable page address for a result being reconstructed. The old frames-based index can be unhelpful; the direct chapter page exposes the reading structure.

NIST-produced federal material is generally public domain in the United States; preserve attribution and separately identified contributions, data, and software terms. Official contents checked on 28 October 2026. The reading remains external.

**Return:** [Measurement and metrology sources — books, guides, and research](../Sources.lingua#r_6H584EJ2Z9D11PG89Y21Y8T1A3).
For clean-manufacturing qualification and monitoring, read [chapter 6](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/pmc_d.htm) through acceptance sampling, charts, time-series methods and the case studies. Selected OC, counts, EWMA, CUSUM, covariance and capability passages were inspected on 30 October 2026. Independent calculations reconstruct the 52-item/3-acceptance OC example, the twenty-observation EWMA sequence and the twenty-five-wafer count summary. The OC table entries at p = 0.04 and 0.05 disagree with ordinary rounding of its formula; the learning Record preserves the checked values. The remaining case studies and experiments were not reproduced. Historical standard citations and rules of thumb are not adopted as universal current requirements.


**Inquiry-core design reading — 31 October 2026:** official sections [5.3.3, choosing a design](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri33.htm), [5.3.3.1, completely randomized designs](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri331.htm), and [5.3.3.2, randomized blocks](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri332.htm), were inspected for the design question, allocation and nuisance-factor discussion. Begin with those arrangements and definitions; inference and ANOVA formulas require later statistical preparation. The new length/time and finite-population examples are original teaching constructions, not reconstructions of the handbook's industrial experiment. This adds selected chapter-5 reading to the existing source identity without claiming the whole chapter was audited.


**Second inquiry-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** rechecked the actual design text in [5.3.3](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri33.htm), [5.3.3.1](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri331.htm) and [5.3.3.2](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri332.htm), and read the definitions/discussion in [7.1.3.1, critical values and p-values](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/prc/section1/prc131.htm). The finite common-core comparison supplies its own null, assignment set, statistic and tie-inclusive tail. Its rejection probability is at most a predeclared threshold; discreteness can make it strictly smaller, so the handbook’s general significance-level wording must not be read as exact equality for this small finite test. No full ANOVA, handbook or instrument-calibration audit is claimed. The new six-unit study is original synthetic content, with its own independently checked calculations.


**Fourth inquiry-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** selected interpretation passages in [1.3.5.2 Confidence Limits for the Mean](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda352.htm) were inspected, including the distinction between repeated-sampling procedure coverage and the fixed observed interval. The common uniform-error example derives its own distribution and coverage; the page's Student-t formula and numerical dataset were not adopted or reconstructed. Normal-model inference, the broader chapter and its exercises remain progressive assignments. Prior chapter/source identity, inspections and federal/third-party rights qualifications remain; no dataset or source media is bundled.


**Fifth inquiry-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** selected official pages [4.1.4.1 linear least squares](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section1/pmd141.htm), [4.4.4 residual assessment](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section4/pmd44.htm) and [4.4.4.6 lack of fit](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section4/pmd446.htm) were inspected for parameter linearity, residual criticism and replicated variation. The common block supplies original coefficient-weight derivations, finite error models and an algebraic replicated partition. It does not adopt the full significance-test formulas without their distributional preparation. The displayed pressure/temperature fit and first data rows were inspected, but no complete source dataset refit or physical calibration was performed. Broader chapters, GUM/Possolo assignments and published numerical reconstruction remain learner work. Existing source identity, historical inspections and federal/third-party rights qualifications are preserved; no source media is bundled.


**Eighth engineering-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** inspected the actual official text of [8.1.3.1 Censoring](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/apr/section1/apr131.htm), [8.1.6.1 Exponential](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/apr/section1/apr161.htm) and [8.4.5.1 Constant repair rate](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/apr/section4/apr451.htm). Selected reading covers exposure, first-failure distributions and the zero-failure calculation. The common study reconstructs the latter page's hypothetical 800-hour zero-failure alternative: 800/[−ln(0.05)] = 267.0466 hours. Its final example omits the minus sign despite the earlier correct formula and positive rounded result; the reconstruction states that correction. This is not raw-data access, a complete table reconstruction or physical reliability evidence. The general chi-square procedures, wider chapter and examples remain assigned reading. Existing handbook identity and federal/third-party rights qualifications are retained; source media remain external.

